==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|gostly}}, {{m|enm|gastlich}}, from {{inh|en|ang|gāstlīċ|t=spiritual, holy, clerical (not lay), ghastly, ghostly, spectral}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|ghost|ly}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|gostly}}, {{m|sco|gastly}}, {{m|sco|gaistlie|t=spiritual, ghastly, terrifying}}, {{cog|fy|geastlik|t=spiritual, clerical, religious}}, {{cog|nl|geestelijk|t=spiritual, clerical, ecclesiastical}}, {{cog|de|geistlich|t=spiritual, sacred, religious}}, {{cog|da|geistlig|t=ecclesiastical, clerical}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|/ˈɡoʊstli/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-ghostly.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Of or pertaining to [[ghost]]s or [[spirit]]s.
#: {{ux|en|a '''ghostly''' figure with a hood}}
#: {{ux|en|The graveyard was haunted by a '''ghostly''' figure of a young girl.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''ghostly''' moaning was heard from upstairs.}}
# [[spooky|Spooky]]; [[frightening]].
#*: {{ux|en|A '''ghostly''' hush fell.}}
# Relating to the [[soul]]; not [[carnal]] or [[secular]]; [[spiritual]].
#: ''a '''ghostly''' confessor''
#* Book of Common Prayer
#*: Save and defend us from our '''ghostly''' enemies.
#* Jeremy Taylor
#*: one of the '''ghostly''' children of St. Jerome

